-- **Symmetry:** The covariance matrix must be symmetric. If the input is not symmetric, the result is incorrect. The implementation assumes symmetry or uses the average of off-diagonal elements [G].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Gradient of 2D Gaussian PDF (Conic)](3472-gradient-of-2d-gaussian-pdf-conic.md)
-Computes the PDF value and its gradients with respect to the mean and covariance parameters. Choose this when training Gaussian splats and backpropagating errors to the primitive parameters.
+Computes the PDF value and its gradients with respect to the mean and covariance parameters. It provides three variants: one including the normalization factor, one excluding it, and one using the conic representation for efficiency. Choose this when training Gaussian splats and backpropagating errors to primitive parameters.
 
 ### [2D Gaussian Probability Density (Conic)](3475-2d-gaussian-probability-density-conic.md)
-Computes the unnormalized PDF using the conic matrix (inverse covariance) and a rescaling factor. Choose this when the conic representation is already available and normalization is handled separately or not required.
+Computes the unnormalized PDF using the conic matrix (inverse covariance) and a rescaling factor. It differs from its siblings by operating directly on the conic representation and including a multiplicative rescaling term [G]. Choose this when the conic representation is already available and normalization is handled separately [G].
 
 ### [Single Point Alpha Forward](3478-single-point-alpha-forward.md)
-Computes the alpha contribution of a single Gaussian splat to a pixel. Choose this when integrating the PDF into the final rasterization pipeline, including camera projection and intrinsic opacity scaling.
+Computes the alpha value for a single 3D Gaussian projected onto a 2D pixel. It determines the opacity contribution of a single Gaussian splat to a pixel during object-order rasterization [G]. It differs from its siblings by including the full projection pipeline and intrinsic opacity scaling, whereas the parent method only evaluates the PDF given a pre-computed 2D covariance [G]. Choose this when integrating the PDF into the final rasterization pipeline [G].
 
